% ============================================================================
% atmosphere-layers — the five layers of Earth's atmosphere and ONE distinctive
% feature the source states for each (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big pictures of earth science, and a rung a WEATHER or SPACE
% agent reasons UP from: the air above you is not one uniform blanket but a
% stack of FIVE layers, surface-upward — troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere,
% thermosphere, exosphere — and each layer is famous for one thing. The
% troposphere is where the weather happens; the stratosphere holds the ozone
% layer; meteors burn up in the mesosphere; the auroras glow in the
% thermosphere; and the exosphere is the highest layer of all. Before you can
% reason about a storm, a satellite, or the northern lights you must first know
% WHICH layer you are in. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? atmosphere_layer_feature(stratosphere, $Feature)   % ozone_layer
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `atmosphere_layer_feature(layer, feature)` carrying the table's
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the feature WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these five layers (it never invents a
% feature).
%
% Each of the five layers and the distinctive feature the source states for it,
% surface-upward, as a little truth table:
%
%     layer          distinctive feature   a beginner's cue
%     ------------   -------------------   ------------------------------------
%     troposphere    weather               the layer we live in; weather + clouds
%     stratosphere   ozone_layer           holds the ozone layer, blocks UV
%     mesosphere     meteors               meteors burn up here ("shooting stars")
%     thermosphere   auroras               the aurora borealis / australis glow here
%                                          (the page also places them in the
%                                          exosphere's lowest part — this table
%                                          gives one feature per layer, and does
%                                          not claim the aurora is unique to one)
%     exosphere      highest               the highest, outermost layer; fades to space
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a feature and recall its layer:
%
%     ? atmosphere_layer_feature($Layer, auroras)   % thermosphere
%
% A word that is NOT one of these five layers — `mantle`, `crust`, `ionosphere`,
% `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so an atmosphere recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a feature. (The `mantle` is a layer of the solid EARTH,
% not of the atmosphere — a classic distractor.) That honest-abstention property
% is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% layer→feature pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `feature` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to echo a
% distinctive feature the source EXPLICITLY states for that layer — never one
% the source does not support. Where the atom compresses the source's phrase it
% keeps the source's own word (the page says "ozone layer", so the atom is
% `ozone_layer`; it says "the highest layer", so the atom is `highest`), so
% every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance. All five pairs come from the SAME primary source, NASA's "Earth's
% Atmosphere: A Multi-layered Cake" (science.nasa.gov), a U.S. government page,
% so `trust authoritative`.
%
% EVERY ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN, below, in the row's `{ ... }` block
% (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e) — read those, not a restatement here. This header used to
% re-quote all five spans a second time, under the words "quoted here
% word-for-word", and TWO of those five occur nowhere on the page: the
% stratosphere one re-opened a mid-sentence clause as its own sentence, and the
% exosphere one ended at a full stop the page does not have. (Not a punctuation
% difference — restoring the page's curly apostrophes still finds neither.) A
% second copy of a quote is a second thing to keep true; the rows are the copy
% that ships, and they are checked against the page.
%
% WHAT #14986 CHANGED HERE. This table had ONE envelope for five rows, and that
% envelope was the TROPOSPHERE row's own weather sentence — so recalling the
% exosphere returned a warrant about clouds. Now each row states its own.
%
% THE SPANS ARE WIDENED, NOT PAIRED. Three of the five features are carried by
% a sentence whose subject is only "here" or "this atmospheric layer". The
% shipped rule (../README.md, "A citation must name its own subject") is to
% WIDEN the quote until it is self-contained, and that is what these rows do:
% each span begins at the last sentence naming its layer before the feature
% sentence. 346 / 227 / 269 / 358 / 194 characters.
%
% The envelope keeps the FRAMING span — the sentence that defends the table as
% a whole, that these are the layers in order. It warrants no row, and a test
% asserts it never reaches an answer.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table atmosphere_layer_feature {
    columns layer, feature

    row (troposphere, weather) {
        % WIDENED to be self-contained. The feature sentence says
        % "happens HERE"; quoted alone it names no layer and grounds
        % nothing. Widened back to the nearest sentence that names
        % the troposphere -- the README rule, not a new invention.
        source "The troposphere is the densest atmospheric layer, compressed by the weight of the rest of the atmosphere above it. Most of Earth’s weather happens here, and almost all clouds that are generated by weather are found here, with the exception of cumulonimbus thunder clouds, whose tops can rise into the lowest parts of the neighboring stratosphere."
    }
    row (stratosphere, ozone_layer) {
        source "Located between approximately 12 and 50 kilometers (7.5 and 31 miles) above Earth’s surface, the stratosphere is perhaps best known as home to Earth’s ozone layer, which protects us from the Sun’s harmful ultraviolet radiation."
    }
    row (mesosphere, meteors) {
        % WIDENED. "Most meteors burn up in THIS ATMOSPHERIC LAYER"
        % names no layer on its own.
        source "The very scarce water vapor present at the top of the mesosphere forms noctilucent clouds, the highest clouds in Earth’s atmosphere, which can be seen by the naked eye under certain conditions and at certain times of day. Most meteors burn up in this atmospheric layer."
    }
    row (thermosphere, auroras) {
        % WIDENED. "...are sometimes seen HERE" names no layer on its
        % own. NOTE the page also places auroras in the exosphere's
        % lowest part; this table carries one feature per layer and
        % does not claim the aurora is unique to the thermosphere.
        source "Located between about 80 and 700 kilometers (50 and 440 miles) above Earth’s surface is the thermosphere, whose lowest part contains the ionosphere. In this layer, temperatures increase with altitude due to the very low density of molecules found here. It is both cloud- and water vapor-free. The aurora borealis and aurora australis are sometimes seen here."
    }
    row (exosphere, highest) {
        % The bounding-language audit flagged `highest` as a
        % superlative with no bounding language in its provenance.
        % The page states it outright. A false alarm, and the second
        % in this cascade after biology/rainforest-layer.
        source "Located between about 700 and 10,000 kilometers (440 and 6,200 miles) above Earth’s surface, the exosphere is the highest layer of Earth’s atmosphere and, at its top, merges with the solar wind."
    }

    % The envelope is the FRAMING span -- what defends the table as a
    % whole: that these are the layers, in order. Until #14986 it was the
    % TROPOSPHERE row's own weather sentence, so a recall of the
    % exosphere was warranted by a sentence about clouds.
    source "From lowest to highest, the major layers are the troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere and exosphere."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-atmosphere/earths-atmosphere-a-multi-layered-cake/"
    trust authoritative
}
